Field report from the instance fix (getwyrd/wyrd-pdca#173 → getwyrd/wyrd-pdca#193, merged; the delta survives the v0.56.0 render — `autoiterate.py` untouched upstream in v0.55/v0.56).

One design warning before this lands here: **the obvious fix regresses round 5.** Making the open-row exclusion symmetric-fuzzy (`any(_same_finding(entry, o) for o in still_open)`) re-creates the permanently-unclearable pair: in the round-5 fixture (`test_an_EXACT_tick_beats_ambiguity`) the still-open near-twin fuzzy-matches the exactly-ticked entry, so the tick retires nothing — every round, forever. The two halves of the guard cannot simply share the fuzzy matcher; the *assignment* has to be exact-first, mirroring the tick match:

- an open row **verbatim equal** to a ledger entry protects *that entry alone* — a sibling row cannot shield its exactly-ticked neighbour, so near-twin pairs still drain;
- an **edited** open row (verbatim owner of nothing) protects **every** entry it `_same_finding`-matches — fail closed, the round-4 doctrine applied to the exclusion: a finding that lingers is visible, a lost one is unrecoverable.

Implementation is a `protected: set[int]` computed once before the tick loop (getwyrd/wyrd-pdca@e4fdf3b, `src/pdca_harness/autoiterate.py`). Three tests port directly:

- the issue's repro: annotated-unticked row + a similar ticked new finding → the entry survives;
- a matcher-drift guard: every edit shape `_same_finding` tolerates in a *tick* must also protect when left *open* — even against an exact tick. Pins the asymmetry itself, so the two matchers cannot drift apart again;
- an edited open row matching two near-twin entries protects both.

All pre-existing round-4/5/6 tests pass unchanged.
